1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,460
A doctor checks blood pressure.

2
00:00:02,460 --> 00:00:04,470
A cyclist checks tyre pressure.

3
00:00:04,470 --> 00:00:07,284
A cook watches the
gauge on a gas cylinder.

4
00:00:07,684 --> 00:00:10,923
For gases, pressure is the
easy number to measure.

5
00:00:10,923 --> 00:00:13,188
Counting moles in a litre is hard.

6
00:00:13,638 --> 00:00:16,829
Aarav pumps his bicycle
tyre and reads the gauge.

7
00:00:16,829 --> 00:00:17,729
Three bar.

8
00:00:17,686 --> 00:00:19,153
Not moles per litre.

9
00:00:19,153 --> 00:00:20,053
Bar.

10
00:00:20,005 --> 00:00:22,706
Last time, K c used concentrations.

11
00:00:22,706 --> 00:00:26,658
For a mixture of gases, can
we use pressures instead?

12
00:00:27,158 --> 00:00:32,070
By the end, you will have K p, a K
made of pressures, and one formula that

13
00:00:32,070 --> 00:00:33,378
links it to K c.

14
00:00:34,028 --> 00:00:36,054
First, a new word: homogeneous.

15
00:00:36,054 --> 00:00:37,911
It means the same all through.

16
00:00:38,261 --> 00:00:39,723
Think of a smoothie.

17
00:00:39,723 --> 00:00:41,782
Everything is blended into one.

18
00:00:41,782 --> 00:00:43,842
You cannot see separate pieces.

19
00:00:44,242 --> 00:00:48,534
In a homogeneous equilibrium, every
reactant and product is in the same

20
00:00:48,534 --> 00:00:49,434
phase.

21
00:00:49,126 --> 00:00:52,068
All gases, or all
dissolved in one solution.

22
00:00:52,668 --> 00:00:57,292
Let's put nitrogen and hydrogen on
our molecule bench, in a closed tank,

23
00:00:57,292 --> 00:00:58,534
to make ammonia.

24
00:00:58,884 --> 00:01:00,468
All three are gases.

25
00:01:00,468 --> 00:01:01,988
They mix completely.

26
00:01:01,988 --> 00:01:02,913
One phase.

27
00:01:02,913 --> 00:01:04,752
A homogeneous equilibrium.

28
00:01:05,152 --> 00:01:07,174
Every molecule hits the walls.

29
00:01:07,174 --> 00:01:08,890
Those hits make pressure.

30
00:01:09,290 --> 00:01:11,458
Each gas pushes with its own share.

31
00:01:11,458 --> 00:01:13,572
That share is its partial pressure.

32
00:01:13,972 --> 00:01:17,834
Add up all the partial pressures,
and you get the total pressure in the

33
00:01:17,834 --> 00:01:18,734
tank.

34
00:01:18,711 --> 00:01:22,767
The same idea works in a solution,
like iron ions and thiocyanate ions,

35
00:01:22,767 --> 00:01:24,219
all dissolved in water.

36
00:01:24,819 --> 00:01:26,184
Now build K p.

37
00:01:26,184 --> 00:01:29,900
Same recipe as K c, but
with partial pressures.

38
00:01:30,250 --> 00:01:33,326
Products on top, reactants
below, each raised to its power.

39
00:01:33,726 --> 00:01:38,771
For ammonia, K p is the pressure of
ammonia squared, over nitrogen times

40
00:01:38,771 --> 00:01:39,991
hydrogen cubed.

41
00:01:39,991 --> 00:01:41,386
Pressures in bar.

42
00:01:42,036 --> 00:01:44,173
How are K p and K c linked?

43
00:01:44,173 --> 00:01:45,282
Step by step.

44
00:01:45,632 --> 00:01:46,546
Step one.

45
00:01:46,546 --> 00:01:48,375
The ideal gas equation.

46
00:01:48,375 --> 00:01:50,003
p V equals n RT.

47
00:01:50,753 --> 00:01:51,673
Step two.

48
00:01:51,673 --> 00:01:52,829
Divide by V.

49
00:01:52,829 --> 00:01:55,779
Pressure equals n over V, times RT.

50
00:01:55,779 --> 00:01:58,438
And n over V is the concentration.

51
00:01:59,188 --> 00:02:00,148
Step three.

52
00:02:00,148 --> 00:02:03,526
So every pressure is its
concentration times RT.

53
00:02:03,526 --> 00:02:05,038
Put that into K p.

54
00:02:05,788 --> 00:02:06,729
Step four.

55
00:02:06,729 --> 00:02:09,520
The RT terms collect into one power.

56
00:02:09,520 --> 00:02:13,284
K p equals K c times RT,
to the power delta n.

57
00:02:14,034 --> 00:02:15,493
What is delta n?

58
00:02:15,843 --> 00:02:20,908
Delta n is moles of gas on the product
side, minus moles of gas on the

59
00:02:20,908 --> 00:02:22,083
reactant side.

60
00:02:22,483 --> 00:02:24,569
For ammonia: two minus four.

61
00:02:24,569 --> 00:02:26,198
Delta n is minus two.

62
00:02:26,598 --> 00:02:29,159
For hydrogen iodide: two minus two.

63
00:02:29,159 --> 00:02:30,059
Zero.

64
00:02:29,743 --> 00:02:31,315
So K p equals K c.

65
00:02:31,715 --> 00:02:37,109
Use R equal to zero point zero eight
three one bar litre per mole per kelvin,

66
00:02:37,109 --> 00:02:38,567
with T in kelvin.

67
00:02:39,167 --> 00:02:40,067
Compare.

68
00:02:40,034 --> 00:02:44,129
Hydrogen iodide: delta
n zero, K p equals K c.

69
00:02:44,129 --> 00:02:49,568
Ammonia: delta n minus two, K
p is K c divided by RT squared.

70
00:02:49,968 --> 00:02:51,214
Pause and think.

71
00:02:51,214 --> 00:02:56,149
For two SO two plus O two making two
SO three, at five hundred kelvin,

72
00:02:56,149 --> 00:02:58,673
is K p bigger or smaller than K c?

73
00:03:01,873 --> 00:03:02,773
Smaller.

74
00:03:02,725 --> 00:03:08,982
Delta n is minus one, so K p is K c
divided by RT, and RT is about forty

75
00:03:08,982 --> 00:03:09,882
two.

76
00:03:10,244 --> 00:03:11,470
Now, the units of K.

77
00:03:11,820 --> 00:03:15,914
K c has units of moles per
litre, to the power delta n.

78
00:03:15,914 --> 00:03:19,248
K p has units of bar,
to the power delta n.

79
00:03:19,648 --> 00:03:22,782
When delta n is zero,
K has no units at all.

80
00:03:23,182 --> 00:03:28,038
In thermodynamics, each value is
divided by its standard value, one mole

81
00:03:28,038 --> 00:03:29,738
per litre or one bar.

82
00:03:29,738 --> 00:03:31,385
Then K has no units.

83
00:03:31,985 --> 00:03:33,190
Remember this.

84
00:03:33,190 --> 00:03:37,110
K p equals K c times RT,
to the power delta n.

85
00:03:37,810 --> 00:03:39,500
Now, one worked example.

86
00:03:39,800 --> 00:03:45,467
For PC l five breaking into PC l
three and chlorine, K c is zero point

87
00:03:45,467 --> 00:03:48,482
zero four zero at five hundred kelvin.

88
00:03:48,482 --> 00:03:49,515
Find K p.

89
00:03:50,015 --> 00:03:50,915
Delta n.

90
00:03:50,798 --> 00:03:53,207
Two moles of gas made, one used.

91
00:03:53,207 --> 00:03:55,149
Two minus one is plus one.

92
00:03:55,899 --> 00:03:59,886
RT is zero point zero eight
three one times five hundred.

93
00:03:59,886 --> 00:04:01,792
Forty one point five five.

94
00:04:02,542 --> 00:04:07,516
K p equals zero point zero four zero
times forty one point five five.

95
00:04:07,516 --> 00:04:09,717
About one point six six bar.

96
00:04:10,467 --> 00:04:16,274
Delta n is plus one, so K p comes out
bigger than K c, with the unit bar.

97
00:04:16,924 --> 00:04:18,385
Now, the exam trap.

98
00:04:18,735 --> 00:04:21,806
Students use R equal to eight
point three one four here.

99
00:04:22,206 --> 00:04:23,825
That R is for joules.

100
00:04:23,825 --> 00:04:27,730
With bar and litres, use zero
point zero eight three one.

101
00:04:27,730 --> 00:04:29,449
And T always in kelvin.

102
00:04:30,099 --> 00:04:31,635
Where is this asked?

103
00:04:31,635 --> 00:04:35,480
Boards ask you to derive the
link between K p and K c.

104
00:04:35,480 --> 00:04:39,046
Entrance tests ask you to
find K p, or its units.

105
00:04:39,646 --> 00:04:40,913
Exam level now.

106
00:04:40,913 --> 00:04:45,874
For nitrogen plus three hydrogen making
two ammonia at five hundred kelvin,

107
00:04:45,874 --> 00:04:49,363
K c is sixty one litre
squared per mole squared.

108
00:04:49,363 --> 00:04:50,308
Find K p.

109
00:04:50,308 --> 00:04:51,629
Pause and solve.

110
00:04:57,029 --> 00:04:58,354
The answer is A.

111
00:04:58,354 --> 00:05:02,433
About zero point zero three
five three bar to the minus two.

112
00:05:02,933 --> 00:05:05,168
Delta n is two minus four.

113
00:05:05,168 --> 00:05:06,186
Minus two.

114
00:05:06,686 --> 00:05:09,205
RT is forty one point five five.

115
00:05:09,205 --> 00:05:11,799
Squared, about seventeen twenty six.

116
00:05:12,299 --> 00:05:17,256
Sixty one divided by seventeen twenty
six is zero point zero three five

117
00:05:17,256 --> 00:05:18,156
three.

118
00:05:17,947 --> 00:05:20,052
C used delta n minus one.

119
00:05:20,652 --> 00:05:22,192
Here is your recap.

120
00:05:22,192 --> 00:05:23,775
Screenshot this card.

121
00:05:26,975 --> 00:05:29,161
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

122
00:05:29,561 --> 00:05:30,461
One.

123
00:05:30,034 --> 00:05:32,308
What makes an equilibrium homogeneous?

124
00:05:33,108 --> 00:05:34,008
Two.

125
00:05:33,599 --> 00:05:37,119
What is delta n when nitrogen
and hydrogen make ammonia?

126
00:05:37,919 --> 00:05:38,819
Three.

127
00:05:38,521 --> 00:05:40,366
When does K p equal K c?

128
00:05:41,266 --> 00:05:44,860
Next time: what if a solid
or a pure liquid takes part?

129
00:05:44,860 --> 00:05:46,225
Does it go into K?

130
00:05:46,225 --> 00:05:48,680
We find out in Heterogeneous Equilibria.
